<h2 id="how">How it works</h2>
<p>Every hour it compares the archive with the tape store, a root at a time. When the archive has moved on, it takes each <a href="docs/dbntape#full-days">full ETH day</a> the store lacks and:</p>
<ol class="steps-list">
    <li><strong>Waits for the whole date.</strong> Only days every schema has reached are used, so a half-collected day never becomes a tape.</li>
    <li><strong>Builds</strong> the date's S1, Trade and L1 tapes, compressed with zstd.</li>
    <li><strong>Proves</strong> them against the archive with the checks in <a href="docs/dbntape#proof">Prove them</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Uploads</strong> them, then records the date in the store, so a restart or a new machine carries on from there.</li>
</ol>
<p>
    A date that fails any step uploads nothing, is logged, and is tried again when the archive next changes. A date
    that hits an error, such as the store going away, is tried again at the next pass, and the dates after it carry
    on. When <code>mbo</code> arrives for a date that was spooled without it, that date is built again with its tick
    tapes. If no <code>mbo</code> has arrived for more than a week, later dates stop waiting for it and get bar tapes
    only. Each root's catalog goes up at the end of its pass.
</p>
<p>
    Holidays, early closes and late opens get no tapes. Each pass also takes off the store, and out of the ledger, any
    tape for such a date that an older version put there, and logs each date with its reason. A catalog that would take
    off more than a tenth of a root's dates is not trusted: nothing is removed, and the pass reports it as a problem.
</p>
<p>
    A date that stops DbnSpool itself, as running out of memory does, is noted in the ledger before its work starts.
    Once a date has stopped it twice, it is set aside and logged, and the dates and roots after it carry on. A set-aside
    date is tried again once DbnSpool's code or its memory limit changes, so raising <code>mem_limit</code> in the
    Compose file gives it another go.
</p>
<p>
    On a first run it spools everything the archive holds, oldest first, which takes many hours. It runs at low priority
    on one thread, and only ever reads the archive, so it can't get in DbnSync's way.
</p>

<h2 id="install">Install on DigitalOcean</h2>
<p>
    These steps add DbnSpool to a <a href="docs/dbnsync#digitalocean">DbnSync Droplet</a>. Use a Droplet with 4 GB of
    memory: the Compose file holds DbnSpool to one CPU and 2 GB, and a heavy trade date peaks at about 1.5 GB while
    it is built and proved. Allow disk for the bars, definitions and statistics it caches: about 12 GB for 12 roots
    from 2020.
</p>
<ol class="steps-list">
    <li><strong>Create a second Space</strong> for tapes, such as <code>dbn-tapes</code>, in the same region. Keep it private, with no CDN.</li>
    <li>
        <strong>Empty a tape Space an older DbnSpool filled.</strong> Tapes used to be one per contract, named like
        <code>ESZ6.s1.20261029.tape</code>; now a date has one of each kind, named like <code>ES.s1.20261029.tape</code>.
        DbnSpool refuses a Space that still holds the old ones, and says so in its log and an alert. With DbnSpool
        stopped, delete everything under <code>GLBX.MDP3/</code> in that Space: the tapes, <code>catalog/</code> and
        <code>spool/</code>. It then builds every date again, which takes days for the full history.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Create a Spaces key</strong> with Limited Access: <em>Read</em> on the archive's Space and
        <em>Read/Write/Delete</em> on the tapes' Space. DbnSpool can never change the archive with it.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Add it to <code>.env</code></strong> on the Droplet, in both pairs of lines. Two separate keys work as well.
        <CodeBlock Title=".env on the Droplet" Text="@SpoolEnv" />
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Start it.</strong>
        <CodeBlock Title="Shell on the Droplet" Text="@SpoolStart" />
    </li>
</ol>
<p>
    Tapes come to about 5 GB a month for 12 roots with <code>mbo</code>, on top of the archive, and both Spaces share
    your Spaces allowance. Spaces is billed by DigitalOcean, not by anything in DbnSuite.
</p>
<div class="note warn"><p>
    <strong>Tapes are derived from data licensed to you.</strong> The tape store must stay private, and the same limits
    apply to it as to the archive. See <a href="docs/dbntape#storage">Storage and licensing</a>.
</p></div>
<p>
    DbnSpool isn't tied to DigitalOcean: <code>Archive__Location</code> and <code>Tapes__Location</code> each take any
    S3 address or a folder.
</p>

<h2 id="read">Read the tapes</h2>
<p>
    Copy tapes down to a fast local drive and replay them from there. Give <code>dbnf</code> a login for the tapes'
    Space with a read-only key, then pull. Files already here are skipped, so running it again fetches only what's new.
</p>
<CodeBlock Title="Any terminal" Text="@Pull" />

<h2 id="watch">Watch it run</h2>
<p>
    Its log goes to Seq, and <code>/status</code> shows the last pass root by root, the roots the running pass has
    finished, and the latest problem, such as a date set aside. Reach it through the tunnel with
    <code>-L 8081:localhost:8081</code>, at <code>http://localhost:8081/status</code>. <code>/health</code> says whether
    it is up.
</p>
<p>
    With the <a href="docs/dbnsync#alert-emails">alert email settings</a> in <code>.env</code>, DbnSpool emails you
    when dates are set aside, tapes can't be made, or a root's pass fails, once a day at most for each problem. Set up
    the <a href="docs/dbnsync#alerts">DigitalOcean alerts</a> as well: they email you when memory or disk runs high, or
    the Droplet stops answering.
</p>

<h2 id="settings">Settings</h2>
<div class="table">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Default</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>Archive__Location</code>, <code>Archive__Bucket</code>, <code>Archive__AccessKey</code>, <code>Archive__SecretKey</code></td><td>none, <code>dbn</code></td><td>The archive to read.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Tapes__Location</code>, <code>Tapes__Bucket</code>, <code>Tapes__AccessKey</code>, <code>Tapes__SecretKey</code></td><td>none, <code>dbn</code></td><td>Where tapes go, which must not be the archive itself. The Compose file sets the bucket to <code>dbn-tapes</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>DbnSpool__Roots</code></td><td>the 12 default roots</td><td>The roots to spool.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>DbnSpool__Interval</code></td><td><code>01:00:00</code></td><td>How long to wait between looks at the archive.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>DbnSpool__Compress</code></td><td><code>true</code></td><td>Compress tapes with zstd.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>DbnSpool__Prove</code></td><td><code>true</code></td><td>Prove each date before uploading it.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>DbnSpool__MostAttempts</code></td><td><code>2</code></td><td>How many times a date may stop DbnSpool before it is set aside.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>DbnSpool__WorkPath</code></td><td><code>/work</code></td><td>The folder for tapes being built and cached archive files.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Alerts__Host</code>, <code>Alerts__Port</code>, <code>Alerts__User</code>, <code>Alerts__Password</code>, <code>Alerts__From</code>, <code>Alerts__To</code></td><td>off, <code>465</code></td><td><a href="docs/dbnsync#alert-emails">Alert emails</a>. The Compose file fills them from the <code>SMTP_</code> and <code>ALERT_</code> lines of <code>.env</code>, with port 2465.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Alerts__Every</code></td><td><code>1.00:00:00</code></td><td>How long before the same problem is emailed again.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

